1
00:00:06,006 --> 00:00:11,211
This course is going
to be running the 13th through

2
00:00:11,211 --> 00:00:17,884
the 17th, Monday through
Friday mornings and afternoons.

3
00:00:17,884 --> 00:00:20,220
What we're going to
be doing is something

4
00:00:20,220 --> 00:00:22,655
that is a little bit
terrifying, which

5
00:00:22,655 --> 00:00:24,958
is attempting to
teach Introduction

6
00:00:24,958 --> 00:00:30,163
to Computational Neuroscience
using a tool that has only very

7
00:00:30,163 --> 00:00:32,232
recently been developed.

8
00:00:32,232 --> 00:00:34,334
Only in the last
couple of years

9
00:00:34,334 --> 00:00:37,337
have we been actually
developing Neuroblox.

10
00:00:37,337 --> 00:00:40,907
We have a beta
version that is now

11
00:00:40,907 --> 00:00:44,244
being released as of this week.

12
00:00:44,244 --> 00:00:46,746
And so you are actually
the first users

13
00:00:46,746 --> 00:00:50,250
who are not the people
who develop the software.

14
00:00:50,250 --> 00:00:55,188
That is kind of
exciting, but also runs

15
00:00:55,188 --> 00:00:58,758
the risk that there may be
bugs that we hadn't anticipated

16
00:00:58,758 --> 00:01:01,094
because you will use the
software in ways that we hadn't

17
00:01:01,094 --> 00:01:02,028
anticipated.

18
00:01:02,028 --> 00:01:03,797
That's all a good thing.

19
00:01:03,797 --> 00:01:07,734
And so rather than fighting
it, I suggest we embrace it.

20
00:01:07,734 --> 00:01:11,471
There is within the
Slack in #general

21
00:01:11,471 --> 00:01:13,606
bookmarked a reported bug.

22
00:01:13,606 --> 00:01:17,210
There's a
difference between I'm

23
00:01:17,210 --> 00:01:19,546
trying to do something and
I don't know how to do it,

24
00:01:19,546 --> 00:01:23,283
versus I know how to do
it and it's not working.

25
00:01:23,283 --> 00:01:26,119
If it's the latter, then
please report the bug so

26
00:01:26,119 --> 00:01:30,457
that we can try to rectify
it as soon as possible.

27
00:01:30,457 --> 00:01:32,592
This is our team.

28
00:01:32,592 --> 00:01:36,096
As you can see, it's
pretty interdisciplinary.

29
00:01:36,096 --> 00:01:39,666
It's also across
multiple institutions.

30
00:01:39,666 --> 00:01:43,136
MIT is obviously very
well represented here.

31
00:01:43,136 --> 00:01:48,174
Members of this team
are going to be some

32
00:01:48,174 --> 00:01:49,642
of them are physically here.

33
00:01:49,642 --> 00:01:53,947
Some of them are monitoring
channels remotely.

34
00:01:53,947 --> 00:01:57,450
But this gives you sort of an
idea of who the main individuals

35
00:01:57,450 --> 00:02:00,019
are so that when I credit
them during the talk,

36
00:02:00,019 --> 00:02:01,521
you kind of understand
who they are.

37
00:02:01,521 --> 00:02:03,656
And this also gives
you a sense of who would

38
00:02:03,656 --> 00:02:04,924
be answering your questions.

39
00:02:07,627 --> 00:02:13,233
If we're going to talk about
Intro to Computational Modeling,

40
00:02:13,233 --> 00:02:15,735
OK, so what is
computational modeling?

41
00:02:15,735 --> 00:02:19,739
Modeling means different
things to different people.

42
00:02:19,739 --> 00:02:23,176
If we're thinking about
a material model--

43
00:02:23,176 --> 00:02:26,946
the father of cybernetics
had a very memorable statement.

44
00:02:26,946 --> 00:02:30,116
"The best material model of a
cat is another or preferably

45
00:02:30,116 --> 00:02:32,385
the same cat."

46
00:02:32,385 --> 00:02:34,888
What does that mean?

47
00:02:34,888 --> 00:02:36,756
It means that if you
want to characterize

48
00:02:36,756 --> 00:02:41,094
all of the features of
a particular system,

49
00:02:41,094 --> 00:02:42,962
your model must be--

50
00:02:42,962 --> 00:02:45,131
essentially, have a
one-to-one mapping

51
00:02:45,131 --> 00:02:47,967
between the features
you're trying to describe

52
00:02:47,967 --> 00:02:51,037
and the components of the model.

53
00:02:51,037 --> 00:02:53,339
But Lemma's point here--

54
00:02:53,339 --> 00:02:57,110
Lemma is the cat, our cat.

55
00:02:57,110 --> 00:03:00,547
Her point is that you run
the risk of overfitting,

56
00:03:00,547 --> 00:03:03,516
depending on what the
aims of the model are.

57
00:03:03,516 --> 00:03:10,256
So if your aim is to answer
every possible question,

58
00:03:10,256 --> 00:03:13,960
then you need to essentially
reproduce the system completely.

59
00:03:13,960 --> 00:03:17,263
But in most cases, we
make strategic choices

60
00:03:17,263 --> 00:03:21,334
about what we're trying to
accomplish with the model.

61
00:03:21,334 --> 00:03:25,004
And so Lemma says, "a
computational model's level

62
00:03:25,004 --> 00:03:27,373
of resolution is a
critical design choice

63
00:03:27,373 --> 00:03:30,243
that needs to match
the system's goals."

64
00:03:30,243 --> 00:03:31,678
I agree with her.

65
00:03:31,678 --> 00:03:34,347
She's a smart cat.

66
00:03:34,347 --> 00:03:38,585
Different computational
neuroscience software platforms

67
00:03:38,585 --> 00:03:40,220
exist.

68
00:03:40,220 --> 00:03:42,055
Some of them are
very well developed

69
00:03:42,055 --> 00:03:45,024
and have accomplished
a tremendous amount.

70
00:03:45,024 --> 00:03:48,561
Neuroblox distinguishes
itself by its specific set

71
00:03:48,561 --> 00:03:50,697
of goals, which is
really optimized

72
00:03:50,697 --> 00:03:53,132
for biomedical
engineering applications.

73
00:03:53,132 --> 00:03:55,301
What we're
primarily interested in

74
00:03:55,301 --> 00:03:58,871
is mechanistic integration of
disorder and treatment-relevant

75
00:03:58,871 --> 00:04:00,306
targets.

76
00:04:00,306 --> 00:04:06,145
And that motivation is informing
all the design decisions

77
00:04:06,145 --> 00:04:09,315
that we made in terms of the
architecture of the software,

78
00:04:09,315 --> 00:04:16,089
what we leave in, what we leave
out, what types of solvers

79
00:04:16,089 --> 00:04:16,756
we use.

80
00:04:16,756 --> 00:04:20,059
All sorts of technical and
also conceptual decisions

81
00:04:20,059 --> 00:04:21,361
are based on that.

82
00:04:21,361 --> 00:04:24,664
If we think about
where our data come from,

83
00:04:24,664 --> 00:04:27,333
they could come all the
way from the bottom patch

84
00:04:27,333 --> 00:04:31,537
clamp all the way to the
clinical signs and symptoms

85
00:04:31,537 --> 00:04:34,507
that we're trying to explain,
so psychiatric and neurological

86
00:04:34,507 --> 00:04:36,042
disorders.

87
00:04:36,042 --> 00:04:40,446
And so you might be
able to ask, all right,

88
00:04:40,446 --> 00:04:42,415
what is causing dementia?

89
00:04:42,415 --> 00:04:45,418
What is the time
course of dementia?

90
00:04:45,418 --> 00:04:48,154
Who is at risk for dementia?

91
00:04:48,154 --> 00:04:51,157
What will be the progression
of that disorder?

92
00:04:51,157 --> 00:04:53,026
Depression.

93
00:04:53,026 --> 00:04:54,961
Addiction.

94
00:04:54,961 --> 00:04:58,464
Many of these are disorders
that are not simply binary.

95
00:05:01,067 --> 00:05:03,236
They have trajectories.

96
00:05:03,236 --> 00:05:07,540
They might be cyclical,
like bipolar disorder.

97
00:05:07,540 --> 00:05:09,842
They might be degenerative.

98
00:05:09,842 --> 00:05:12,912
They might have an
onset, for example,

99
00:05:12,912 --> 00:05:16,783
with psychotic disorders
that start in men at one age

100
00:05:16,783 --> 00:05:18,117
and at women at another.

101
00:05:18,117 --> 00:05:20,353
And you'd want to
understand why.

102
00:05:20,353 --> 00:05:22,221
And so what we want
to do is to create

103
00:05:22,221 --> 00:05:24,991
a model that is
capable of addressing

104
00:05:24,991 --> 00:05:26,826
those sorts of questions.

105
00:05:26,826 --> 00:05:29,495
In order to address
those sorts of questions,

106
00:05:29,495 --> 00:05:32,565
we're going to have to ask
in terms of the modeling

107
00:05:32,565 --> 00:05:37,270
level, what are the
targets potentially

108
00:05:37,270 --> 00:05:41,908
at all scales that might give
rise to these emergent effects?

109
00:05:41,908 --> 00:05:44,477
That means that
we're going to need

110
00:05:44,477 --> 00:05:48,715
to be able to include
components within our model that

111
00:05:48,715 --> 00:05:53,119
have something to do with ion
channels, receptors, neurons,

112
00:05:53,119 --> 00:05:58,725
neural masses, brain regions,
all the way up to the circuits

113
00:05:58,725 --> 00:06:03,496
that we associate with the
macroscopic neuroimaging world.

114
00:06:03,496 --> 00:06:07,467
Ultimately, we output to
clinical signs and symptoms

115
00:06:07,467 --> 00:06:10,370
everything from
behavioral outputs

116
00:06:10,370 --> 00:06:12,105
in terms of how
someone might perform

117
00:06:12,105 --> 00:06:15,808
on a task, but also
mood, for example,

118
00:06:15,808 --> 00:06:19,078
if we want to chart
bipolar disorder.

119
00:06:19,078 --> 00:06:27,320
What this means then is that
if the mechanistic targets are

120
00:06:27,320 --> 00:06:29,789
at all of these
scales, the treatments

121
00:06:29,789 --> 00:06:32,525
also might be operating
at many different scales.

122
00:06:32,525 --> 00:06:35,528
So we want to be able
to, within our model,

123
00:06:35,528 --> 00:06:39,132
implement different
pharmaceutical treatments

124
00:06:39,132 --> 00:06:41,033
and ask, what would
be the impact of,

125
00:06:41,033 --> 00:06:45,171
for example, in this
case, giving L-dopa?

126
00:06:45,171 --> 00:06:47,840
We might want to
ask about metabolism

127
00:06:47,840 --> 00:06:51,644
in the context of brain aging
and Alzheimer's disease,

128
00:06:51,644 --> 00:06:54,647
where in general, the field
believes that metabolism has

129
00:06:54,647 --> 00:06:57,617
a really critical role to play.

130
00:06:57,617 --> 00:07:02,555
But what is the pathway by
which this metabolic dysfunction

131
00:07:02,555 --> 00:07:04,791
actually then affects
brain function?

132
00:07:04,791 --> 00:07:09,395
And specifically, why some brain
functions rather than others?

133
00:07:09,395 --> 00:07:13,900
And then finally, we might want
to be able to ask of the model,

134
00:07:13,900 --> 00:07:16,836
tell us something about what
happens when we stimulate

135
00:07:16,836 --> 00:07:18,571
the system in various ways.

136
00:07:18,571 --> 00:07:21,307
So if we administer
deep brain stimulation

137
00:07:21,307 --> 00:07:24,477
or transcranial
magnetic stimulation,

138
00:07:24,477 --> 00:07:28,314
we're influencing the system
in some particular way.

139
00:07:28,314 --> 00:07:30,283
And bear in mind
that the influence,

140
00:07:30,283 --> 00:07:32,385
the direction of influence
is not just upward.

141
00:07:32,385 --> 00:07:34,987
There are also
feedback effects that

142
00:07:34,987 --> 00:07:36,622
are working downwards as well.

143
00:07:36,622 --> 00:07:41,661
So we need to consider the
causality from the bottom layer

144
00:07:41,661 --> 00:07:43,830
all the way to the top
layer, but then also,

145
00:07:43,830 --> 00:07:45,965
how elements of the top
layer feed back down

146
00:07:45,965 --> 00:07:48,367
to the bottom layer.

147
00:07:48,367 --> 00:07:50,269
The final point
I want to mention

148
00:07:50,269 --> 00:07:52,738
is that if, in
fact, what we want

149
00:07:52,738 --> 00:07:58,377
to get out of this is something
like homeostatic regulation

150
00:07:58,377 --> 00:08:04,550
or behavior on a behavioral
task, then our outputs have to,

151
00:08:04,550 --> 00:08:08,488
in some sense, they have
to be able to do a task.

152
00:08:08,488 --> 00:08:11,591
The model needs to
be able to do a task.

153
00:08:11,591 --> 00:08:16,596
It needs to not just say,
this person will do it well,

154
00:08:16,596 --> 00:08:17,997
this person will not do it well.

155
00:08:17,997 --> 00:08:23,002
We want the model to actually
elicit the task behavior

156
00:08:23,002 --> 00:08:26,606
that a human would perform.

157
00:08:26,606 --> 00:08:30,409
So that imposes all
sorts of constraints.

158
00:08:30,409 --> 00:08:32,512
But it's good, and
actually I would

159
00:08:32,512 --> 00:08:36,649
say critical, to have those
ends in mind before you start.

160
00:08:36,649 --> 00:08:40,386
I should also mention that a
lot of the models that you're

161
00:08:40,386 --> 00:08:42,522
going to be working with
don't take advantage

162
00:08:42,522 --> 00:08:44,857
of all the capabilities
we're developing,

163
00:08:44,857 --> 00:08:48,694
because as an introduction to
computational neuroscience, what

164
00:08:48,694 --> 00:08:53,299
we're trying to do is to show
you how the tool can be used

165
00:08:53,299 --> 00:08:56,435
with existing models, which are
canonical and very important

166
00:08:56,435 --> 00:08:57,670
within the field.

167
00:08:57,670 --> 00:09:00,039
And then we'll
give you an example

168
00:09:00,039 --> 00:09:02,942
of how Neuroblox can be applied
to the sorts of models

169
00:09:02,942 --> 00:09:05,578
that we're describing here.

170
00:09:05,578 --> 00:09:11,150
In the world of neuroimaging,
which is where I come from,

171
00:09:11,150 --> 00:09:13,953
there are terms that get
tossed around a lot: "networks",

172
00:09:13,953 --> 00:09:16,656
"circuits", "computational model".

173
00:09:16,656 --> 00:09:19,258
And that's true.

174
00:09:19,258 --> 00:09:21,794
I mean, these are real entities.

175
00:09:21,794 --> 00:09:25,164
These are important
areas of research

176
00:09:25,164 --> 00:09:28,167
that have yielded really
important insights.

177
00:09:28,167 --> 00:09:30,403
But different fields
have different ways

178
00:09:30,403 --> 00:09:32,171
of understanding the same words.

179
00:09:32,171 --> 00:09:34,540
And so for the
sake of clarity, I

180
00:09:34,540 --> 00:09:36,242
want to be kind of
clear about what

181
00:09:36,242 --> 00:09:39,178
we mean by these
things in this context.

182
00:09:39,178 --> 00:09:41,447
In the context
of neuroimaging,

183
00:09:41,447 --> 00:09:47,687
when we say "networks", and
even sometimes "circuits",

184
00:09:47,687 --> 00:09:49,956
what is typically
meant is something

185
00:09:49,956 --> 00:09:54,427
that looks like this, where
we have a seed region,

186
00:09:54,427 --> 00:09:58,464
and then we're asking, what
other areas of the brain

187
00:09:58,464 --> 00:10:01,567
are co-activated with
that seed region?

188
00:10:01,567 --> 00:10:04,437
So what we're really doing
is taking a time series

189
00:10:04,437 --> 00:10:07,607
from one region and asking
for the time series that

190
00:10:07,607 --> 00:10:10,676
are obtained from every
other voxel in the brain

191
00:10:10,676 --> 00:10:12,745
or every other
region in the brain,

192
00:10:12,745 --> 00:10:15,014
depending on how you
parsellate your brain.

193
00:10:15,014 --> 00:10:19,552
The areas that are associated
with that first region that

194
00:10:19,552 --> 00:10:24,090
are somehow connected or
communicating or signaling

195
00:10:24,090 --> 00:10:30,496
are described in that way based
on correlation coefficients.

196
00:10:30,496 --> 00:10:33,366
You can also expand this to
a general linear model, where

197
00:10:33,366 --> 00:10:35,935
you have a design
matrix and you ask,

198
00:10:35,935 --> 00:10:40,006
in response to some particular
experimental input: what areas

199
00:10:40,006 --> 00:10:44,076
are activated in response to
that input, differentially,

200
00:10:44,076 --> 00:10:46,646
let's say, between one
condition versus another,

201
00:10:46,646 --> 00:10:48,781
between one population
versus another?

202
00:10:48,781 --> 00:10:50,449
But these are all
statistical tests.

203
00:10:54,186 --> 00:10:58,824
When we talk about computational
modeling or computational

204
00:10:58,824 --> 00:11:02,161
neuroscience within the
neuroimaging world, what

205
00:11:02,161 --> 00:11:06,666
is often meant is
really something very,

206
00:11:06,666 --> 00:11:09,635
very different than how basic
neuroscientists would think

207
00:11:09,635 --> 00:11:11,937
of computational neuroscience.

208
00:11:11,937 --> 00:11:15,608
What's happening [in neuroimaging] is that
there is an emphasis, really,

209
00:11:15,608 --> 00:11:19,612
on developing tools
that are able to make

210
00:11:19,612 --> 00:11:22,682
better use of the
time series that

211
00:11:22,682 --> 00:11:25,551
are obtained from these voxels.

212
00:11:25,551 --> 00:11:29,455
The sorts of
computational tools that

213
00:11:29,455 --> 00:11:31,257
have been developed
in neuroimaging,

214
00:11:31,257 --> 00:11:33,559
by and large (there
are exceptions),

215
00:11:33,559 --> 00:11:37,363
really, they hinge around
two different concepts.

216
00:11:37,363 --> 00:11:40,866
One is improving reliability and
reproducibility of connectivity,

217
00:11:40,866 --> 00:11:43,569
which as we said,
are correlations,

218
00:11:43,569 --> 00:11:46,772
co-activation patterns,
which are described

219
00:11:46,772 --> 00:11:50,476
through the general linear
model, or feature extraction,

220
00:11:50,476 --> 00:11:52,211
often through
independent components

221
00:11:52,211 --> 00:11:57,183
analysis using meta analyses
across data sets and modalities.

222
00:11:57,183 --> 00:12:00,619
Given what has
sometimes been described

223
00:12:00,619 --> 00:12:04,090
as a reproducibility crisis
within the fMRI world,

224
00:12:04,090 --> 00:12:07,026
there's a very strong
effort to see, OK,

225
00:12:07,026 --> 00:12:09,495
if we say these networks
exist for one data set,

226
00:12:09,495 --> 00:12:11,597
do they also exist
for other data sets?

227
00:12:11,597 --> 00:12:13,966
If we say they exist
for fMRI, can we

228
00:12:13,966 --> 00:12:16,435
elicit them also from MEG?

229
00:12:16,435 --> 00:12:23,576
So the idea here is really
to use computational power

230
00:12:23,576 --> 00:12:27,913
to enhance this reproducibility
and reliability.

231
00:12:27,913 --> 00:12:31,650
The second context
is using some methods

232
00:12:31,650 --> 00:12:35,554
from machine learning to
decode functions and disorders

233
00:12:35,554 --> 00:12:37,056
from time series.

234
00:12:37,056 --> 00:12:39,225
And in this case,
what's really happening

235
00:12:39,225 --> 00:12:42,528
is that people are identifying
signatures, brain signatures,

236
00:12:42,528 --> 00:12:45,397
for this disorder
versus that disorder,

237
00:12:45,397 --> 00:12:48,934
or for this function
versus that function.

238
00:12:48,934 --> 00:12:53,272
And here, the advantage
is that decoding actually

239
00:12:53,272 --> 00:12:55,708
can be done not
just on time series,

240
00:12:55,708 --> 00:12:57,643
but on lots of
different types of data.

241
00:12:57,643 --> 00:13:00,813
So you can use multimodal data
and use structural information,

242
00:13:00,813 --> 00:13:03,883
functional information,
all sorts of information

243
00:13:03,883 --> 00:13:09,054
together to provide these
sorts of diagnostic tools.

244
00:13:12,191 --> 00:13:16,595
But for the purposes of what
I've said in terms of our aims,

245
00:13:16,595 --> 00:13:20,065
those two approaches
are not what we mean

246
00:13:20,065 --> 00:13:23,736
by computational neuroscience.

247
00:13:23,736 --> 00:13:26,872
For one thing,
what we need is

248
00:13:26,872 --> 00:13:32,812
to be able to simulate
the impact of one drug

249
00:13:32,812 --> 00:13:36,081
or device versus another,
to tune parameters

250
00:13:36,081 --> 00:13:41,086
and ask how the system will
change, to ask how a patient who

251
00:13:41,086 --> 00:13:43,956
has undergone deep
brain stimulation

252
00:13:43,956 --> 00:13:46,826
is likely to respond
to tuning parameters

253
00:13:46,826 --> 00:13:48,594
this way versus that way.

254
00:13:48,594 --> 00:13:50,896
All of this requires simulation.

255
00:13:50,896 --> 00:13:53,399
This requires an
element of time.

256
00:13:53,399 --> 00:13:56,302
So it's not just asking: 
are you in this category

257
00:13:56,302 --> 00:13:57,570
or this category?

258
00:13:57,570 --> 00:14:01,807
It's asking: how does the
system evolve over time?

259
00:14:01,807 --> 00:14:05,744
Both of these methods
that I've described

260
00:14:05,744 --> 00:14:07,847
are discriminative.

261
00:14:07,847 --> 00:14:09,348
You can't simulate with them.

262
00:14:09,348 --> 00:14:11,217
You can describe symptoms.

263
00:14:11,217 --> 00:14:14,386
You can describe disorders.

264
00:14:14,386 --> 00:14:16,155
You can describe functions.

265
00:14:16,155 --> 00:14:18,157
But what you can't
do is simulate

266
00:14:18,157 --> 00:14:21,694
how they will evolve over time.

267
00:14:21,694 --> 00:14:24,797
In terms of interpretability,
machine learning

268
00:14:24,797 --> 00:14:28,534
has its strengths precisely
because it's high dimensional

269
00:14:28,534 --> 00:14:29,568
in this context.

270
00:14:29,568 --> 00:14:31,103
There are types of
machine learning,

271
00:14:31,103 --> 00:14:32,404
of course, that are generative.

272
00:14:32,404 --> 00:14:34,306
But that's not what's
being generally applied

273
00:14:34,306 --> 00:14:37,276
to neuroimaging data.

274
00:14:37,276 --> 00:14:39,578
But because it's
high dimensional,

275
00:14:39,578 --> 00:14:43,015
the flip side to that is
that it's not interpretable.

276
00:14:43,015 --> 00:14:46,185
So you can't take those
signatures elicited

277
00:14:46,185 --> 00:14:48,621
by machine learning
from neuroimaging data

278
00:14:48,621 --> 00:14:55,127
and then reverse engineer what
actually the brain is doing.

279
00:14:55,127 --> 00:14:58,697
Likewise, using multiple
pairwise correlations

280
00:14:58,697 --> 00:15:02,835
to infer circuit architecture
is not very reliable

281
00:15:02,835 --> 00:15:05,704
when you're looking at networks.

282
00:15:05,704 --> 00:15:10,576
For one thing,
circuits include feedback,

283
00:15:10,576 --> 00:15:14,446
and correlations fall
apart when you're

284
00:15:14,446 --> 00:15:18,450
looking at systems
that include feedback.

285
00:15:18,450 --> 00:15:21,987
The second point is
that in a network,

286
00:15:21,987 --> 00:15:24,556
you have pairwise
correlations, which

287
00:15:24,556 --> 00:15:28,260
if you add them-- so if you have
a particular network with nodes

288
00:15:28,260 --> 00:15:31,430
and you ask, what's the
correlation between this node

289
00:15:31,430 --> 00:15:33,432
and this node, and then
this node and this node,

290
00:15:33,432 --> 00:15:40,205
and this node and this node, the
error estimates for each edge

291
00:15:40,205 --> 00:15:42,574
multiply.

292
00:15:42,574 --> 00:15:44,710
And as a result,
when you ask, what

293
00:15:44,710 --> 00:15:49,615
is the error of a
single connection,

294
00:15:49,615 --> 00:15:51,317
the error for a
single connection

295
00:15:51,317 --> 00:15:52,985
might be totally manageable.

296
00:15:52,985 --> 00:15:56,588
But once you get to a
network with many edges,

297
00:15:56,588 --> 00:16:00,693
you have this exponential
amplification of error.

298
00:16:00,693 --> 00:16:02,962
It can very quickly
get out of control.

299
00:16:02,962 --> 00:16:06,598
And actually, if you start
with an R value, so a Pearson

300
00:16:06,598 --> 00:16:10,769
correlation of
0.45, which is quite

301
00:16:10,769 --> 00:16:12,438
strong within
neuroimaging, you'd

302
00:16:12,438 --> 00:16:16,308
be super happy if you
got such a correlation.

303
00:16:16,308 --> 00:16:20,312
But that's only explaining
20% of the variance, which

304
00:16:20,312 --> 00:16:25,050
means 80% of the variance
is not being explained.

305
00:16:25,050 --> 00:16:29,254
Now, if you have
three steps, you

306
00:16:29,254 --> 00:16:34,526
have 0.2 cubed, which means
that you're not explaining

307
00:16:34,526 --> 00:16:37,296
over 99% of the variance.

308
00:16:37,296 --> 00:16:39,031
That's not a very good model.

309
00:16:39,031 --> 00:16:44,003
So for that reason,
it's not a great way

310
00:16:44,003 --> 00:16:48,207
to identify the
structure of circuits.

311
00:16:48,207 --> 00:16:50,576
The other problem
is that even if you

312
00:16:50,576 --> 00:16:54,313
were doing a great job
of absolutely identifying

313
00:16:54,313 --> 00:16:57,449
the structure of the
circuit with your networks,

314
00:16:57,449 --> 00:17:00,953
the other issue is
that just talking

315
00:17:00,953 --> 00:17:03,956
about who's connected
to whom is a very

316
00:17:03,956 --> 00:17:06,725
sparse amount of information
about the structure

317
00:17:06,725 --> 00:17:07,826
of your model.

318
00:17:07,826 --> 00:17:09,895
And so as we'll
see, there are a lot

319
00:17:09,895 --> 00:17:13,298
of functions, really critical
functions within the brain, that

320
00:17:13,298 --> 00:17:17,169
require a lot more information
than who is talking to whom.

321
00:17:20,839 --> 00:17:23,142
So if that's not
what we're using,

322
00:17:23,142 --> 00:17:28,180
if we're not going to be
just relying on connectivity,

323
00:17:28,180 --> 00:17:32,151
connectivity understood as
correlation coefficients

324
00:17:32,151 --> 00:17:36,622
or general linear models,
then what we'll be using

325
00:17:36,622 --> 00:17:39,725
is something called
dynamical systems,

326
00:17:39,725 --> 00:17:42,327
which are based on ODE systems.

327
00:17:42,327 --> 00:17:45,230
The advantage
of ODE systems is

328
00:17:45,230 --> 00:17:47,900
that they address a lot of the
concerns that I just raised.

329
00:17:47,900 --> 00:17:50,903
You can talk about the
error of the system

330
00:17:50,903 --> 00:17:54,406
as a whole in a way that
you can't with a series

331
00:17:54,406 --> 00:17:55,641
of correlations.

332
00:17:55,641 --> 00:17:57,776
You can model
explicitly feedback

333
00:17:57,776 --> 00:18:00,779
and also, you can simulate.

334
00:18:00,779 --> 00:18:04,650
And so here, I take as a really
simple example glucose insulin

335
00:18:04,650 --> 00:18:05,818
regulation.

336
00:18:05,818 --> 00:18:09,288
This is something that
probably all of you know about.

337
00:18:09,288 --> 00:18:10,355
You eat a cookie.

338
00:18:10,355 --> 00:18:12,691
Your glucose levels go up.

339
00:18:12,691 --> 00:18:15,694
But glucose levels being
too high is quite dangerous.

340
00:18:15,694 --> 00:18:21,934
And so your endocrine system
has a feedback loop that

341
00:18:21,934 --> 00:18:24,403
allows you to release insulin.

342
00:18:24,403 --> 00:18:27,106
Insulin then lowers
your glucose levels.

343
00:18:27,106 --> 00:18:31,376
If your glucose levels go too
low, then you release glucagon

344
00:18:31,376 --> 00:18:33,178
and the glucose levels go up.

345
00:18:33,178 --> 00:18:36,515
So here you have a
homeostatic regulatory system

346
00:18:36,515 --> 00:18:40,586
that maintains glucose within
appropriate thresholds.

347
00:18:40,586 --> 00:18:45,624
And so what you can see here
is if we give a glucose bolus,

348
00:18:45,624 --> 00:18:49,461
we can simulate how glucose
and insulin will respond.

349
00:18:49,461 --> 00:18:50,963
So glucose will respond first.

350
00:18:50,963 --> 00:18:53,765
Insulin will respond
shortly thereafter.

351
00:18:53,765 --> 00:18:55,367
And then glucose will go down.

352
00:18:55,367 --> 00:18:57,035
And then insulin will go down.

353
00:18:57,035 --> 00:19:02,407
You can see that one of the nice
features of this kind of model

354
00:19:02,407 --> 00:19:07,179
is that if we say that the
circuit is dysregulated,

355
00:19:07,179 --> 00:19:11,416
we can be kind of precise
about how it gets dysregulated.

356
00:19:11,416 --> 00:19:13,452
And it turns out
that different ways

357
00:19:13,452 --> 00:19:15,654
of dysregulating
the same circuit

358
00:19:15,654 --> 00:19:19,391
will lead to very
different phenotypes.

359
00:19:19,391 --> 00:19:24,796
In Type 1 diabetes,
unmedicated Type 1 diabetics

360
00:19:24,796 --> 00:19:26,965
tend to be very, very
thin because insulin

361
00:19:26,965 --> 00:19:31,003
is responsible for putting
on body fat and storing it.

362
00:19:31,003 --> 00:19:34,039
Type 2 diabetics
who are unmedicated

363
00:19:34,039 --> 00:19:38,277
will tend to be
overweight because they

364
00:19:38,277 --> 00:19:42,447
have too much insulin, and
they're storing the glucose

365
00:19:42,447 --> 00:19:44,750
rather than consuming it.

366
00:19:44,750 --> 00:19:48,053
So if you were to just look at
individuals with Type 1 and Type

367
00:19:48,053 --> 00:19:50,722
2 diabetes and just look
at those phenotypes,

368
00:19:50,722 --> 00:19:52,958
you wouldn't
necessarily understand

369
00:19:52,958 --> 00:19:56,895
that those two disorders,
which look completely different

370
00:19:56,895 --> 00:19:59,064
from a clinical
perspective, actually

371
00:19:59,064 --> 00:20:01,233
represent different
types of dysregulation

372
00:20:01,233 --> 00:20:03,368
of the very same circuit.

373
00:20:03,368 --> 00:20:06,872
That is actually
kind of provocative

374
00:20:06,872 --> 00:20:09,041
when we think about
psychiatric disorders,

375
00:20:09,041 --> 00:20:10,809
because psychiatric
disorders tend

376
00:20:10,809 --> 00:20:15,581
to implicate many of the same
circuits, in spite of the fact

377
00:20:15,581 --> 00:20:17,282
that [their clinical presentations] can look
quite different.

378
00:20:17,282 --> 00:20:19,618
And so this provides
a way of thinking

379
00:20:19,618 --> 00:20:21,086
for how that might occur.

380
00:20:24,990 --> 00:20:29,761
Importantly, also, I've just
done a hypothetical experiment

381
00:20:29,761 --> 00:20:30,262
here.

382
00:20:30,262 --> 00:20:31,263
I've given glucose.

383
00:20:31,263 --> 00:20:34,066
I've seen how there's
a response over time.

384
00:20:34,066 --> 00:20:36,368
But what's very nice about
being able to simulate

385
00:20:36,368 --> 00:20:39,838
is I can ask, all right,
if we chronically do this,

386
00:20:39,838 --> 00:20:42,140
how will the system
change over time?

387
00:20:42,140 --> 00:20:45,010
We can ask about
longer term trajectories.

388
00:20:45,010 --> 00:20:47,212
So when we do
experiments in the lab,

389
00:20:47,212 --> 00:20:48,747
whether we're doing
it in the scanner

390
00:20:48,747 --> 00:20:51,116
or we're doing it with
electrophysiology,

391
00:20:51,116 --> 00:20:54,553
we have a very small
time window in which

392
00:20:54,553 --> 00:20:56,455
we can conduct our experiments.

393
00:20:56,455 --> 00:21:02,261
But clinically, disorders,
diseases, they evolve sometimes

394
00:21:02,261 --> 00:21:03,562
over decades.

395
00:21:03,562 --> 00:21:06,565
And so understanding
how that process occurs

396
00:21:06,565 --> 00:21:10,335
is something that we
can do with simulations.

397
00:21:10,335 --> 00:21:14,973
One example here is
with Type 2 diabetes.

398
00:21:14,973 --> 00:21:17,209
If there's a chronically
elevated demand for insulin,

399
00:21:17,209 --> 00:21:19,211
beta cells hit their
carrying capacity.

400
00:21:19,211 --> 00:21:24,316
After a certain point, the
beta cells, they're fatigued.

401
00:21:24,316 --> 00:21:26,885
They can't keep up
with the demand.

402
00:21:26,885 --> 00:21:30,555
And as a result, Type 2
diabetics can also become Type

403
00:21:30,555 --> 00:21:32,190
1-like diabetics.

404
00:21:32,190 --> 00:21:34,459
Again, this provides
a way of thinking

405
00:21:34,459 --> 00:21:37,162
about certain types of
psychiatric disorders

406
00:21:37,162 --> 00:21:42,234
that seem to flip states,
so for example, from mania

407
00:21:42,234 --> 00:21:48,173
to depression, all sorts
of states from anxiety

408
00:21:48,173 --> 00:21:49,274
to depression.

409
00:21:49,274 --> 00:21:52,711
There are all sorts
of trajectory changes

410
00:21:52,711 --> 00:21:55,147
that occur over much
longer periods of time

411
00:21:55,147 --> 00:21:57,416
than you can measure
in a laboratory

412
00:21:57,416 --> 00:21:59,217
that you'd want to
be able to explain

413
00:21:59,217 --> 00:22:03,155
with your computational models.

414
00:22:03,155 --> 00:22:06,024
And note that when I
say carrying capacity,

415
00:22:06,024 --> 00:22:09,828
in the context of neuroscience,
this is a very real concern,

416
00:22:09,828 --> 00:22:11,830
because a lot of
these dysregulations

417
00:22:11,830 --> 00:22:15,901
are putting a tremendous amount
of excess stress on the system.

418
00:22:15,901 --> 00:22:22,874
And so if you are in a state,
like mania, where you're rapidly

419
00:22:22,874 --> 00:22:26,645
depleting dopamine, then
what you're going to do

420
00:22:26,645 --> 00:22:31,249
is to put a lot of stress
on precursors for dopamine,

421
00:22:31,249 --> 00:22:34,753
like tyrosine, for example,
where acetylcholine

422
00:22:34,753 --> 00:22:36,355
has a precursor choline.

423
00:22:36,355 --> 00:22:40,726
And what can happen is that as
you hit that carrying capacity,

424
00:22:40,726 --> 00:22:43,061
the system can collapse.

425
00:22:43,061 --> 00:22:46,231
This is a really important
dynamic, overshoot and collapse,

426
00:22:46,231 --> 00:22:48,633
that you'll want to be keeping
in mind for the models.

427
00:22:51,536 --> 00:22:54,873
Finally, I just want to
really bring home, again,

428
00:22:54,873 --> 00:22:59,444
the idea that what I've just
described, the story of being

429
00:22:59,444 --> 00:23:02,547
able to regulate between
glucose and insulin

430
00:23:02,547 --> 00:23:06,318
and to be able to dysregulate in
more than one way, none of this

431
00:23:06,318 --> 00:23:08,820
can be captured
through just asking,

432
00:23:08,820 --> 00:23:10,055
who is connected to whom?

433
00:23:14,025 --> 00:23:17,896
So if we want to
ask, all right, well,

434
00:23:17,896 --> 00:23:22,501
I said, it's not just about
excitatory and inhibitory

435
00:23:22,501 --> 00:23:23,635
responses.

436
00:23:23,635 --> 00:23:25,737
We might need more structure.

437
00:23:25,737 --> 00:23:30,509
So here's an example of this
kind of modeling applied

438
00:23:30,509 --> 00:23:34,780
to the neuroimaging scale.

439
00:23:34,780 --> 00:23:39,284
And we can look at this
prefrontal limbic circuit.

440
00:23:39,284 --> 00:23:41,453
Again, there are
many similarities

441
00:23:41,453 --> 00:23:44,656
with what we've just seen
between the glucose insulin

442
00:23:44,656 --> 00:23:45,791
regulation.

443
00:23:45,791 --> 00:23:48,994
One of them is that we
have not one feedback loop,

444
00:23:48,994 --> 00:23:51,363
but multiple feedback
loops that are all

445
00:23:51,363 --> 00:23:52,931
interacting with one another.

446
00:23:52,931 --> 00:23:55,967
And that's one reason why you
need to simulate the results,

447
00:23:55,967 --> 00:23:58,270
because these are complex
enough that you can't just

448
00:23:58,270 --> 00:24:01,139
imagine, well, if I were
to do this, this would happen,

449
00:24:01,139 --> 00:24:02,974
if I were to do that,
this would happen.

450
00:24:02,974 --> 00:24:04,509
These are feedback
loops that are

451
00:24:04,509 --> 00:24:08,513
operating at multiple scales.
So, at the bottom scale

452
00:24:08,513 --> 00:24:09,714
affecting the top scale.

453
00:24:09,714 --> 00:24:12,717
But also, at each scale you
have multiple feedback loops

454
00:24:12,717 --> 00:24:15,520
interacting with each other.

455
00:24:15,520 --> 00:24:19,291
I also want to point
out that one feature

456
00:24:19,291 --> 00:24:24,729
of this particular model is the
importance of gating mechanisms.

457
00:24:24,729 --> 00:24:26,932
There are several
gating mechanisms here.

458
00:24:26,932 --> 00:24:30,802
One is the thalamus, which is
important for sensory gating,

459
00:24:30,802 --> 00:24:36,975
but also through the IFG in
terms of other types of gating,

460
00:24:36,975 --> 00:24:40,312
determining whether
there's object recognition

461
00:24:40,312 --> 00:24:42,380
or not, and that
affects the feedback

462
00:24:42,380 --> 00:24:45,116
through the ventral stream.

463
00:24:45,116 --> 00:24:48,520
Having these gating
mechanisms, thresholds,

464
00:24:48,520 --> 00:24:50,789
and also timing
issues, the question

465
00:24:50,789 --> 00:24:53,758
of whether some signals are
faster or slower than others

466
00:24:53,758 --> 00:24:55,660
is going to affect
the dynamics as well.

467
00:24:55,660 --> 00:25:00,165
This is something, again,
that ODE systems can provide.

468
00:25:00,165 --> 00:25:06,004
So like Type 1 and
Type 2 diabetics,

469
00:25:06,004 --> 00:25:11,142
depending on how the
system disregulates,

470
00:25:11,142 --> 00:25:14,646
determines the output
in terms of behavior.

471
00:25:14,646 --> 00:25:18,416
Here we were looking
at a largish sample

472
00:25:18,416 --> 00:25:23,021
of 256 individuals: all
the way from clinically

473
00:25:23,021 --> 00:25:26,191
anxious to clinically reckless
and everything in between,

474
00:25:26,191 --> 00:25:28,793
and seeing if we could
map where on the spectrum

475
00:25:28,793 --> 00:25:33,298
someone would lie based on the
particular type of dysregulation

476
00:25:33,298 --> 00:25:33,932
and its degree.

477
00:25:37,969 --> 00:25:43,909
Now, what I just showed you
should be a little bit--

478
00:25:43,909 --> 00:25:47,078
this should shock you a
little bit because I just

479
00:25:47,078 --> 00:25:49,347
talked about gating mechanisms.

480
00:25:49,347 --> 00:25:52,417
And yet, this model,
it has filters.

481
00:25:52,417 --> 00:25:54,019
It has comparators.

482
00:25:54,019 --> 00:25:56,254
It has gains and so forth.

483
00:25:56,254 --> 00:25:59,291
And for those of you
who are neuroscientists,

484
00:25:59,291 --> 00:26:01,159
you are probably asking,
well, wait a second.

485
00:26:01,159 --> 00:26:04,362
I mean, does the brain really
have comparators and gains?

486
00:26:04,362 --> 00:26:05,964
And is that real?

487
00:26:05,964 --> 00:26:09,200
Or is that just a way to
make the simulation work?

488
00:26:09,200 --> 00:26:13,138
This is exactly
the question that we

489
00:26:13,138 --> 00:26:15,240
want to be able to address.

490
00:26:15,240 --> 00:26:18,877
So the question really is, what
are the computational primitives

491
00:26:18,877 --> 00:26:20,045
of brain circuits?

492
00:26:20,045 --> 00:26:22,747
When we say that the
brain is "processing",

493
00:26:22,747 --> 00:26:25,350
what is doing the processing?

494
00:26:25,350 --> 00:26:28,086
Again, it's not just that
this is connected to this

495
00:26:28,086 --> 00:26:30,121
and this is connected to that.

496
00:26:30,121 --> 00:26:31,890
That's not going to
process anything.

497
00:26:31,890 --> 00:26:34,025
So what is doing
that processing?

498
00:26:34,025 --> 00:26:36,294
In the case of an
electrical circuit,

499
00:26:36,294 --> 00:26:40,098
we know what those
computational primitives are.

500
00:26:40,098 --> 00:26:41,766
These are synthetic circuits.

501
00:26:41,766 --> 00:26:43,268
We have capacitors.

502
00:26:43,268 --> 00:26:44,002
We have gains.

503
00:26:44,002 --> 00:26:46,237
We have resistors and so forth.

504
00:26:46,237 --> 00:26:49,874
And so the question
is, even though

505
00:26:49,874 --> 00:26:52,177
in computational
neuroscience sometimes

506
00:26:52,177 --> 00:26:54,279
we use analogs for
those, we'd like

507
00:26:54,279 --> 00:26:57,649
to know that those analogs have
an actual underlying biological

508
00:26:57,649 --> 00:27:00,018
basis.

509
00:27:00,018 --> 00:27:03,455
In Neuroblox,
those are represented.

510
00:27:03,455 --> 00:27:07,459
These underlying processing
elements at the bottom scale

511
00:27:07,459 --> 00:27:10,395
are described as
computational primitives,

512
00:27:10,395 --> 00:27:12,230
biomimetic
computational primitives

513
00:27:12,230 --> 00:27:16,034
because they're meant to
reflect actual brain processes.

514
00:27:16,034 --> 00:27:19,104
And here's an example
of one of them.

515
00:27:19,104 --> 00:27:22,807
This is lateral inhibition.

516
00:27:22,807 --> 00:27:24,909
And if you see how
this works, so you

517
00:27:24,909 --> 00:27:27,579
have a number of
excitatory inputs.

518
00:27:27,579 --> 00:27:32,317
And the first one that
hits inhibits the rest.

519
00:27:32,317 --> 00:27:36,988
I just want to point
out something first.

520
00:27:36,988 --> 00:27:39,958
Again, this is not just
who's connected to whom.

521
00:27:39,958 --> 00:27:43,261
There's a critical timing issue.

522
00:27:43,261 --> 00:27:46,831
The first one that
hits inhibits the rest.

523
00:27:46,831 --> 00:27:50,902
So any model that doesn't
consider the timing element,

524
00:27:50,902 --> 00:27:54,005
it would be missing something
critical about this function.

525
00:27:54,005 --> 00:27:57,509
And second, this is acting
as a type of filter.

526
00:27:57,509 --> 00:27:59,210
And depending on
how you tune it,

527
00:27:59,210 --> 00:28:00,779
it can have different effects.

528
00:28:00,779 --> 00:28:05,684
But it is one potential
mechanism for a filter.

529
00:28:05,684 --> 00:28:08,720
So one of the tasks
that we've had

530
00:28:08,720 --> 00:28:10,555
in Neuroblox is
figuring out, what

531
00:28:10,555 --> 00:28:12,390
are these computational
primitives?

532
00:28:12,390 --> 00:28:14,426
These computational
primitives are mostly

533
00:28:14,426 --> 00:28:16,194
at the spiking neuron scale.

534
00:28:16,194 --> 00:28:18,997
And they're really
micro assemblies.

535
00:28:18,997 --> 00:28:22,600
They're very tiny
structures that have

536
00:28:22,600 --> 00:28:24,436
a very particular function.

537
00:28:24,436 --> 00:28:28,073
And the way we define them is
the smallest possible structure--

538
00:28:28,073 --> 00:28:30,442
the smallest possible
structure that can

539
00:28:30,442 --> 00:28:32,410
perform a particular function.

540
00:28:32,410 --> 00:28:36,347
Here is a sample of these
potential computational

541
00:28:36,347 --> 00:28:37,215
primitives.

542
00:28:37,215 --> 00:28:39,617
These are not made
up out of our heads.

543
00:28:39,617 --> 00:28:43,588
These are coming from
data, from the literature.

544
00:28:43,588 --> 00:28:47,092
They need to be figured out.

545
00:28:47,092 --> 00:28:50,562
And then from these
computational primitives,

546
00:28:50,562 --> 00:28:55,767
we can arrange
these microcircuits

547
00:28:55,767 --> 00:29:00,171
in ways that allow us to
design macro circuits.

548
00:29:00,171 --> 00:29:05,343
So in other words, you have
a bunch of micro circuits

549
00:29:05,343 --> 00:29:07,378
that arrange to create
a macro circuit.

550
00:29:07,378 --> 00:29:09,347
Then that can be
treated as a block that

551
00:29:09,347 --> 00:29:12,984
then is used to arrange
a larger macro circuit.

552
00:29:12,984 --> 00:29:16,988
And this is how
Neuroblox grows in scale.

553
00:29:16,988 --> 00:29:19,891
It's circuits of
circuits of circuits

554
00:29:19,891 --> 00:29:21,459
of circuits all the way up.

555
00:29:25,964 --> 00:29:29,067
Here's an example of
this particular circuit.

556
00:29:29,067 --> 00:29:34,172
And as you can see, so this is
the corticostriatal circuit.

557
00:29:34,172 --> 00:29:37,642
And it's doing a
particular task.

558
00:29:37,642 --> 00:29:41,012
So you might recall that I
said that one of our desiderata

559
00:29:41,012 --> 00:29:46,651
is we want our model to be
actually capable of producing

560
00:29:46,651 --> 00:29:47,786
some effect.

561
00:29:47,786 --> 00:29:49,921
So we want it to be
able to do a task.

562
00:29:49,921 --> 00:29:53,591
And in this case, it's going
through a series of steps.

563
00:29:53,591 --> 00:29:56,795
This is, again, all the way
starting from the spiking neuron

564
00:29:56,795 --> 00:30:00,532
scale, all the way up
to what will be measured

565
00:30:00,532 --> 00:30:02,667
with the local field
potential scale,

566
00:30:02,667 --> 00:30:06,938
and then through
that to behavior.

567
00:30:06,938 --> 00:30:10,675
So these different components
that were assembled

568
00:30:10,675 --> 00:30:13,011
were assembled from
the literature.

569
00:30:13,011 --> 00:30:15,346
So this is a kind
of an integration

570
00:30:15,346 --> 00:30:20,118
of many, many decades
worth of experiments.

571
00:30:20,118 --> 00:30:25,056
Data, models all got
normalized into this Neuroblox

572
00:30:25,056 --> 00:30:28,226
type of nomenclature, and
then combined together

573
00:30:28,226 --> 00:30:31,629
to create this comprehensive
corticostriatal model that

574
00:30:31,629 --> 00:30:33,097
can do category learning.

575
00:30:33,097 --> 00:30:36,467
We then independently
validated against

576
00:30:36,467 --> 00:30:39,871
macaque electrophysiology.

577
00:30:39,871 --> 00:30:41,973
Now again, the
only reason we can

578
00:30:41,973 --> 00:30:45,276
do that is that it's
producing simulations,

579
00:30:45,276 --> 00:30:47,378
so predicted outputs
as trajectories

580
00:30:47,378 --> 00:30:51,416
at multiple scales, spiking
neuron, local field potential,

581
00:30:51,416 --> 00:30:52,417
and behavioral.

582
00:30:52,417 --> 00:30:55,687
And it's being checked and
validated at multiple scales

583
00:30:55,687 --> 00:30:57,956
to make sure that this
is actually correct.

584
00:31:01,025 --> 00:31:05,230
Now, this brings me
to the next question

585
00:31:05,230 --> 00:31:08,633
which is, the user
experience, the GUI,

586
00:31:08,633 --> 00:31:11,002
how do we interact with this?

587
00:31:11,002 --> 00:31:13,271
What we want is,
again, to create

588
00:31:13,271 --> 00:31:17,141
a tool for
neuroscientists, of course,

589
00:31:17,141 --> 00:31:22,080
but also for neuroscience to be
actually extended to developing

590
00:31:22,080 --> 00:31:23,982
better drugs and devices.

591
00:31:23,982 --> 00:31:26,985
So that means that it
can't be the domain

592
00:31:26,985 --> 00:31:31,189
of a small select group
of hard core computational

593
00:31:31,189 --> 00:31:34,726
neuroscientists who live
in a cave somewhere.

594
00:31:34,726 --> 00:31:36,594
It needs to be
something accessible

595
00:31:36,594 --> 00:31:39,797
to many different types of
populations: experimentalists,

596
00:31:39,797 --> 00:31:41,499
theoreticians, and so forth.

597
00:31:41,499 --> 00:31:44,135
And so we really thought
this through very carefully,

598
00:31:44,135 --> 00:31:46,204
again, with those aims in mind.

599
00:31:46,204 --> 00:31:50,008
The tool that I
used with my team

600
00:31:50,008 --> 00:31:53,144
to derive that
fMRI scale control

601
00:31:53,144 --> 00:31:54,846
circuit for the
prefrontal limbic circuit

602
00:31:54,846 --> 00:31:56,314
was done in Simulink.

603
00:31:56,314 --> 00:31:59,550
But Simulink, it
was really developed

604
00:31:59,550 --> 00:32:01,085
for electrical engineering.

605
00:32:01,085 --> 00:32:03,254
It has computational
primitives that

606
00:32:03,254 --> 00:32:06,124
are really specific to
electrical engineering, which

607
00:32:06,124 --> 00:32:11,896
means that it has the advantage
of being able to allow you

608
00:32:11,896 --> 00:32:16,434
to model in a graphical
way, which allows you to tap

609
00:32:16,434 --> 00:32:19,904
into your intuitions, your
visual intuitions, about what

610
00:32:19,904 --> 00:32:21,139
a circuit looks like.

611
00:32:21,139 --> 00:32:24,776
It also allows you to
work at multiple scales,

612
00:32:24,776 --> 00:32:27,679
so you can have blocks
within your Simulink

613
00:32:27,679 --> 00:32:31,449
that are made up of other
circuits underneath it.

614
00:32:31,449 --> 00:32:34,319
So it has many similarities
to what we're trying to do.

615
00:32:34,319 --> 00:32:36,321
But again, this was
optimized specifically

616
00:32:36,321 --> 00:32:37,922
for electrical engineering.

617
00:32:37,922 --> 00:32:42,593
And we want to make sure
that the tool doesn't--

618
00:32:42,593 --> 00:32:44,829
it's sort of optimized
for the sorts of questions

619
00:32:44,829 --> 00:32:49,367
neuroscientists would want to
be asking and also, ideally

620
00:32:49,367 --> 00:32:53,071
also keeps you from
making mistakes

621
00:32:53,071 --> 00:32:55,873
that Simulink would
allow you to make simply

622
00:32:55,873 --> 00:33:00,645
because it doesn't have
biological constraints built in.

623
00:33:00,645 --> 00:33:07,285
So one of the practical problems
we ran into very quickly

624
00:33:07,285 --> 00:33:09,554
is that when we're
looking at circuits

625
00:33:09,554 --> 00:33:13,524
and if you want to map
this onto a brain, which

626
00:33:13,524 --> 00:33:16,561
is the geography that
neuroscientists are

627
00:33:16,561 --> 00:33:20,131
familiar with, the problem is
that circuits are usually not

628
00:33:20,131 --> 00:33:21,833
all in one slice.

629
00:33:21,833 --> 00:33:26,738
If we take one
particular slice,

630
00:33:26,738 --> 00:33:28,606
we're going to have to
be a little bit loosey

631
00:33:28,606 --> 00:33:32,643
goosey about putting the
blocks where they belong.

632
00:33:32,643 --> 00:33:36,047
because we're going to
have cortical areas,

633
00:33:36,047 --> 00:33:39,317
subcortical areas, the front,
the back, the side and side.

634
00:33:39,317 --> 00:33:41,519
And so we had to
come up with a way

635
00:33:41,519 --> 00:33:43,554
of doing this that would
allow the entire circuit

636
00:33:43,554 --> 00:33:45,089
to be visible at once.

637
00:33:45,089 --> 00:33:50,028
And so inspired by a Mercator
projection map, we thought, OK,

638
00:33:50,028 --> 00:33:52,930
can we actually create a
flat map representation

639
00:33:52,930 --> 00:33:54,966
of the entire brain?

640
00:33:54,966 --> 00:33:59,504
And we were able to make
use of some wonderful work

641
00:33:59,504 --> 00:34:05,009
by Hahn and Duckworth in
2023 that did exactly that.

642
00:34:05,009 --> 00:34:08,212
And we were able
to adapt from this.

643
00:34:08,212 --> 00:34:13,217
So here is the GUI
that Neuroblox uses.

644
00:34:13,217 --> 00:34:15,486
And just to walk you
through how to work

645
00:34:15,486 --> 00:34:20,825
your way through this GUI,
the blocks, which include

646
00:34:20,825 --> 00:34:23,327
computational
primitives, but then

647
00:34:23,327 --> 00:34:27,265
it also includes the blocks
that are these circuits that

648
00:34:27,265 --> 00:34:29,000
are created, the
macro circuits that

649
00:34:29,000 --> 00:34:31,302
are created from these
computational primitives.

650
00:34:31,302 --> 00:34:33,938
And so then, again, the
blocks of blocks of blocks

651
00:34:33,938 --> 00:34:36,107
can also be included
as blocks, which

652
00:34:36,107 --> 00:34:38,342
means that when someone
creates a circuit,

653
00:34:38,342 --> 00:34:41,179
they are using the same blocks.

654
00:34:41,179 --> 00:34:45,049
The advantage also, by the way,
in having different groups work

655
00:34:45,049 --> 00:34:48,786
from the same common
set of elements

656
00:34:48,786 --> 00:34:50,688
is that it
accomplishes something

657
00:34:50,688 --> 00:34:52,824
that all too often
doesn't happen

658
00:34:52,824 --> 00:34:55,426
in computational neuroscience,
which is the ability

659
00:34:55,426 --> 00:34:59,397
to compare one model to another
and to integrate models.

660
00:34:59,397 --> 00:35:01,299
So computational
neuroscience tends

661
00:35:01,299 --> 00:35:04,669
to be a very artisanal
activity, where every group has

662
00:35:04,669 --> 00:35:09,841
its own ways of defining the
circuits and their interactions,

663
00:35:09,841 --> 00:35:13,144
their interaction rules,
the ODE solvers even.

664
00:35:13,144 --> 00:35:19,016
And so the advantage of having
this common set of elements that

665
00:35:19,016 --> 00:35:22,086
can be combined is like
in electrical engineering,

666
00:35:22,086 --> 00:35:25,690
if someone says resistor,
I know what resistor means.

667
00:35:25,690 --> 00:35:27,658
I can change parameters
for the resistor.

668
00:35:27,658 --> 00:35:30,061
I can create another
type of resistor.

669
00:35:30,061 --> 00:35:32,396
But a resistor has
a common meaning,

670
00:35:32,396 --> 00:35:34,165
where multiple
groups can combine

671
00:35:34,165 --> 00:35:39,303
circuits, electrical
circuits, in meaningful ways.

672
00:35:39,303 --> 00:35:42,340
But by the way, I should mention
you can create your own blocks.

673
00:35:42,340 --> 00:35:45,109
It's not as if that you are
stuck with the blocks that

674
00:35:45,109 --> 00:35:46,177
already are there.

675
00:35:46,177 --> 00:35:48,346
You can use your own data
or other people's data

676
00:35:48,346 --> 00:35:51,549
to create new blocks.

677
00:35:51,549 --> 00:35:57,388
And then you have your blocks
are arranged on the brain.

678
00:35:57,388 --> 00:36:01,259
So I don't know if you can
see, but these are actually

679
00:36:01,259 --> 00:36:01,859
brain regions.

680
00:36:04,529 --> 00:36:07,632
Can everybody see the brain?

681
00:36:07,632 --> 00:36:08,232
Yeah.

682
00:36:08,232 --> 00:36:10,635
So this is a flat brain that
includes different brain

683
00:36:10,635 --> 00:36:12,136
regions.

684
00:36:12,136 --> 00:36:15,806
And so you actually put your
block on the brain region.

685
00:36:15,806 --> 00:36:18,509
If you select the brain
region, it will automatically

686
00:36:18,509 --> 00:36:20,845
put the block where it belongs.

687
00:36:20,845 --> 00:36:24,182
And the reason why we want to
put things where they belong

688
00:36:24,182 --> 00:36:26,184
is partially because,
again, we have

689
00:36:26,184 --> 00:36:30,821
as neuroscientists certain
intuitions about the brain,

690
00:36:30,821 --> 00:36:31,322
right?

691
00:36:31,322 --> 00:36:33,024
If we see something
in the visual cortex,

692
00:36:33,024 --> 00:36:34,225
we know what that means.

693
00:36:34,225 --> 00:36:37,295
If it's in the striatum,
we know what that means.

694
00:36:37,295 --> 00:36:39,630
So we want to have
things where they belong.

695
00:36:39,630 --> 00:36:43,768
But there are also other
important advantages

696
00:36:43,768 --> 00:36:50,041
of having there be a spatial
geography that is meaningful.

697
00:36:50,041 --> 00:36:52,109
And one of them is
that you can actually

698
00:36:52,109 --> 00:36:55,213
have mechanistic
maps that overlay

699
00:36:55,213 --> 00:36:58,516
with this map, this brain.

700
00:36:58,516 --> 00:37:06,524
And so if you have, for example,
you have different GLUT4 density

701
00:37:06,524 --> 00:37:08,593
distributions
throughout the brain,

702
00:37:08,593 --> 00:37:10,561
if you know what
the GLUT4 density

703
00:37:10,561 --> 00:37:13,931
distribution is throughout the
brain, you can map it onto here.

704
00:37:13,931 --> 00:37:15,967
And that will change the
underlying parameters

705
00:37:15,967 --> 00:37:17,335
of how your model is running.

706
00:37:20,004 --> 00:37:24,408
The last thing I just wanted to
point out is these connections.

707
00:37:24,408 --> 00:37:28,613
So this is arranged on the left
between inputs and outputs.

708
00:37:28,613 --> 00:37:36,053
So the inputs can be drugs,
devices, stimuli, anything

709
00:37:36,053 --> 00:37:38,122
that is actually going
to go into the system.

710
00:37:40,958 --> 00:37:43,461
And then you have
what comes out of it.

711
00:37:43,461 --> 00:37:45,129
And so what comes
out of it could

712
00:37:45,129 --> 00:37:48,099
be various ways of
visualizing your data,

713
00:37:48,099 --> 00:37:51,569
but also, a motor
action, for example.

714
00:37:51,569 --> 00:37:54,839
So if the simulation is
conducting a particular task

715
00:37:54,839 --> 00:37:57,508
in response to some
particular type of stimulus,

716
00:37:57,508 --> 00:38:02,647
the output would be
the behavioral output.

717
00:38:02,647 --> 00:38:05,316
This is being
adapted to a couple

718
00:38:05,316 --> 00:38:07,218
of different applications.

719
00:38:07,218 --> 00:38:09,987
One of these is a Pharma Module.

720
00:38:09,987 --> 00:38:15,559
One of the things we want
to be able to do is to ask,

721
00:38:15,559 --> 00:38:19,797
if we have different
drugs, how are they

722
00:38:19,797 --> 00:38:21,966
affecting not only
neurotransmitter levels,

723
00:38:21,966 --> 00:38:24,735
but also receptor dynamics?

724
00:38:24,735 --> 00:38:27,738
We need to be able to
model receptor dynamics.

725
00:38:27,738 --> 00:38:31,409
It means that when you tag
a particular connection,

726
00:38:31,409 --> 00:38:34,345
you will identify what
neurotransmitter that is.

727
00:38:34,345 --> 00:38:36,180
And then you have
the opportunity

728
00:38:36,180 --> 00:38:39,016
to include relevant
receptor dynamics.

729
00:38:39,016 --> 00:38:41,485
Obviously, at this
level is going

730
00:38:41,485 --> 00:38:43,621
to affect the
computational primitives,

731
00:38:43,621 --> 00:38:46,957
which means that then those
computational primitives will

732
00:38:46,957 --> 00:38:49,627
affect the running of
the circuit as a whole.

733
00:38:49,627 --> 00:38:52,263
And so when you ask, what
is the impact of giving

734
00:38:52,263 --> 00:38:55,333
this drug versus that drug,
at this dose versus that dose,

735
00:38:55,333 --> 00:39:00,071
it's affecting this
level, which is then

736
00:39:00,071 --> 00:39:03,107
going to be affecting the
computational primitives.

737
00:39:03,107 --> 00:39:05,076
And then the
computational primitives

738
00:39:05,076 --> 00:39:08,245
are going to have emergent
effects all the way up

739
00:39:08,245 --> 00:39:09,313
to the behavioral scale.

740
00:39:11,849 --> 00:39:15,886
We also have a Metabolic Module.

741
00:39:15,886 --> 00:39:20,257
This is composed of metabolic
flux equations that,

742
00:39:20,257 --> 00:39:22,960
for those of you who do
magnetic resonance spectroscopy,

743
00:39:22,960 --> 00:39:28,799
allows you to ask, how
will changing metabolism

744
00:39:28,799 --> 00:39:31,068
of this parameter
versus that parameter,

745
00:39:31,068 --> 00:39:35,072
how will it change the behavior
of the system as a whole,

746
00:39:35,072 --> 00:39:38,342
even at the level of
changing someone's diet,

747
00:39:38,342 --> 00:39:41,679
putting someone in
ketosis versus not?

748
00:39:41,679 --> 00:39:43,714
Giving someone Type
2 diabetes, how

749
00:39:43,714 --> 00:39:47,251
is that going to
affect the entire--

750
00:39:47,251 --> 00:39:51,155
so this will affect the
system, the metabolic system.

751
00:39:51,155 --> 00:39:54,091
But then this outputs
neurotransmitters.

752
00:39:54,091 --> 00:39:56,160
Again, those
neurotransmitters are

753
00:39:56,160 --> 00:39:57,762
going to go into that circuit.

754
00:39:57,762 --> 00:40:00,464
And then the circuit
will run its simulations

755
00:40:00,464 --> 00:40:01,832
and have some output.

756
00:40:01,832 --> 00:40:04,969
And that allows us to ask a
whole new category of questions

757
00:40:04,969 --> 00:40:11,675
like, why is it that GLP1
agonists seem to curb addiction?

758
00:40:11,675 --> 00:40:13,844
Well, in order to
answer that question,

759
00:40:13,844 --> 00:40:17,148
we're going to need to be able
to model the metabolic system

760
00:40:17,148 --> 00:40:23,087
and also ask how it impacts
the neural circuits, including

761
00:40:23,087 --> 00:40:24,488
behavior.

762
00:40:24,488 --> 00:40:29,393
And so one of the things
that we're very excited about

763
00:40:29,393 --> 00:40:33,631
is the ability to not only
model circuits within the brain,

764
00:40:33,631 --> 00:40:37,067
but also the brain's
interactions with other systems,

765
00:40:37,067 --> 00:40:39,603
for example, the immune system.

766
00:40:39,603 --> 00:40:42,606
If you've ever thought about
long COVID and its impact

767
00:40:42,606 --> 00:40:45,142
on brain fog, again,
these are really

768
00:40:45,142 --> 00:40:49,313
important clinical
questions to ask.

769
00:40:49,313 --> 00:40:51,982
And then we have also a
Neuromodulation Module

770
00:40:51,982 --> 00:40:55,219
in progress, where we're
interested in being

771
00:40:55,219 --> 00:40:58,556
able to stimulate the
circuit in various ways

772
00:40:58,556 --> 00:41:03,627
with DBS or transcranial
magnetic stimulation

773
00:41:03,627 --> 00:41:07,131
and ask, OK, so how is this
affecting the system as a whole

774
00:41:07,131 --> 00:41:10,067
and its behavior over time?

775
00:41:10,067 --> 00:41:16,307
So finally, I want to say
that one of the grand goals

776
00:41:16,307 --> 00:41:19,610
is to develop Neuroblox
as a knowledge integration

777
00:41:19,610 --> 00:41:23,481
ecosystem in a way that I don't
see existing platforms have kind

778
00:41:23,481 --> 00:41:25,850
of quite gotten us there yet.

779
00:41:25,850 --> 00:41:30,387
And one of them really
has to do with allowing

780
00:41:30,387 --> 00:41:34,625
for the ability of
community-based modeling,

781
00:41:34,625 --> 00:41:37,194
where if one team
is contributing

782
00:41:37,194 --> 00:41:39,697
data and another team
is contributing models

783
00:41:39,697 --> 00:41:42,166
and another team is
contributing other models,

784
00:41:42,166 --> 00:41:46,704
that there can be an integration
and comparison of the models

785
00:41:46,704 --> 00:41:50,274
to the data, the models to
each other in a way, as I said,

786
00:41:50,274 --> 00:41:52,076
that's only possible
if you're working

787
00:41:52,076 --> 00:41:56,514
from a common set
of modular elements.

788
00:41:56,514 --> 00:42:00,084
All of this gets
powered by Julia

789
00:42:00,084 --> 00:42:04,855
and facilitated this
hosting for this week,

790
00:42:04,855 --> 00:42:08,526
but probably also long-term
will be through JuliaHub.

791
00:42:08,526 --> 00:42:11,228
Obviously, this is very
computationally expensive.

792
00:42:11,228 --> 00:42:14,331
And so the
capabilities that Julia

793
00:42:14,331 --> 00:42:16,367
offers in terms of high
performance computing

794
00:42:16,367 --> 00:42:17,902
are really critical.

795
00:42:17,902 --> 00:42:22,139
Neuroblox allows for
simulations and model fitting.

796
00:42:22,139 --> 00:42:24,074
The modularity
that I've described

797
00:42:24,074 --> 00:42:26,877
is designed from the
ground up for automating

798
00:42:26,877 --> 00:42:31,615
a lot of the processes that
otherwise wouldn't be possible:

799
00:42:31,615 --> 00:42:35,786
creating models,
testing models, fine tuning

800
00:42:35,786 --> 00:42:37,988
models, parameter fitting,
comparison of models,

801
00:42:37,988 --> 00:42:42,426
et cetera, global parameter
setting, so again,

802
00:42:42,426 --> 00:42:45,129
putting together a whole
bunch of different models,

803
00:42:45,129 --> 00:42:46,997
asking how they
integrate, and then being

804
00:42:46,997 --> 00:42:49,667
able to change parameters
for the whole system

805
00:42:49,667 --> 00:42:54,338
from one place, and model
comparison and integration.

806
00:42:54,338 --> 00:43:00,311
And now, Helmut Strey, who's
our lead software engineer,

807
00:43:00,311 --> 00:43:01,812
will take over.

808
00:43:01,812 --> 00:43:04,048
I wanted to spend
a few more minutes

809
00:43:04,048 --> 00:43:07,952
on the more technical
aspects of Neuroblox.

810
00:43:07,952 --> 00:43:11,555
In particular, we want to
talk about why we chose Julia

811
00:43:11,555 --> 00:43:15,092
over other [programming languages].

812
00:43:15,092 --> 00:43:18,629
And to start, I
wanted to introduce

813
00:43:18,629 --> 00:43:22,099
what is coined the
"two-language problem"

814
00:43:22,099 --> 00:43:24,268
in computational science.

815
00:43:24,268 --> 00:43:30,207
And that has to do with if you
want to do something performant,

816
00:43:30,207 --> 00:43:33,944
you want to have a low-level
programming language that

817
00:43:33,944 --> 00:43:36,647
is really fast, right?

818
00:43:36,647 --> 00:43:39,950
And that's for execution
speed, because you don't really

819
00:43:39,950 --> 00:43:42,886
want to have a slow simulation,
because typically, you

820
00:43:42,886 --> 00:43:45,189
want to push the envelope.

821
00:43:45,189 --> 00:43:49,393
But on the other hand, if you
have a lower level language,

822
00:43:49,393 --> 00:43:53,664
it's very hard to develop code.

823
00:43:53,664 --> 00:43:57,835
And to increase
development speed,

824
00:43:57,835 --> 00:44:00,004
you need a higher
level language.

825
00:44:00,004 --> 00:44:02,272
And so typically
what people do is,

826
00:44:02,272 --> 00:44:04,908
and this is even true in
computational neuroscience,

827
00:44:04,908 --> 00:44:10,347
is that typically, they use C or
C++ for really performant code,

828
00:44:10,347 --> 00:44:13,884
and then maybe use Python to
interface with that C code.

829
00:44:13,884 --> 00:44:15,986
There are libraries
that essentially

830
00:44:15,986 --> 00:44:19,957
say, build me a neuron,
build me a neuron assembly,

831
00:44:19,957 --> 00:44:23,127
and then that gets
translated into C.

832
00:44:23,127 --> 00:44:25,396
So the nice thing is that
Julia actually solves

833
00:44:25,396 --> 00:44:28,532
these two-language problems
because it is actually

834
00:44:28,532 --> 00:44:31,001
the motto of Julia, is:
"Julia walks like Python

835
00:44:31,001 --> 00:44:34,772
and runs like C." It's
a compiled language.

836
00:44:34,772 --> 00:44:37,708
And it actually, if you
look at performance, there

837
00:44:37,708 --> 00:44:42,513
are online Julia
performance benchmarks,

838
00:44:42,513 --> 00:44:45,149
it's very much like
C, sometimes actually

839
00:44:45,149 --> 00:44:50,387
will beat C in performance
because it's better optimized.

840
00:44:50,387 --> 00:44:52,656
But that's the first thing.

841
00:44:52,656 --> 00:44:54,091
That was our first decision.

842
00:44:54,091 --> 00:44:57,594
The other decision is on
performance and accuracy

843
00:44:57,594 --> 00:45:01,265
of simulation of
differential equations.

844
00:45:01,265 --> 00:45:04,702
Julia, I think, has--

845
00:45:04,702 --> 00:45:08,272
and I think it's pretty--

846
00:45:08,272 --> 00:45:13,510
it's a true statement
that Julia probably

847
00:45:13,510 --> 00:45:16,547
has the best performance
differential equation

848
00:45:16,547 --> 00:45:23,620
solvers in the whole field,
and not only ODE solvers,

849
00:45:23,620 --> 00:45:27,391
but also SDE solvers and delayed
differential equation solvers,

850
00:45:27,391 --> 00:45:31,562
and actually also differential
algebraic equation solvers.

851
00:45:31,562 --> 00:45:35,666
And here are some benchmarks
that I want to show you.

852
00:45:35,666 --> 00:45:38,869
So these are non-stiff
solvers and stiff solvers.

853
00:45:38,869 --> 00:45:42,473
And one of the things that
you will see immediately

854
00:45:42,473 --> 00:45:48,245
is that Julia performs about
two orders of magnitude

855
00:45:48,245 --> 00:45:53,484
faster than Python or Matlab,
which is not super surprising,

856
00:45:53,484 --> 00:45:55,986
because Matlab and
Python are not very high

857
00:45:55,986 --> 00:45:58,055
performance languages.

858
00:45:58,055 --> 00:46:00,824
And they perform very,
very, almost equal

859
00:46:00,824 --> 00:46:03,660
to any implementation
that you can do in C.

860
00:46:03,660 --> 00:46:06,029
So this was one of the reasons.

861
00:46:06,029 --> 00:46:08,198
I don't think there's
any other language that

862
00:46:08,198 --> 00:46:11,201
has so many choices in solvers.

863
00:46:11,201 --> 00:46:15,305
And that's one of
our motivations

864
00:46:15,305 --> 00:46:18,041
that when people
use the software,

865
00:46:18,041 --> 00:46:20,110
they can actually pick
the right solvers.

866
00:46:20,110 --> 00:46:23,947
And this is why I'm actually
showing non-stiff versus stiff.

867
00:46:23,947 --> 00:46:25,816
Non-stiff are
differential equations

868
00:46:25,816 --> 00:46:29,887
where the functions
are relatively smooth

869
00:46:29,887 --> 00:46:32,055
and nice and well-behaved.

870
00:46:32,055 --> 00:46:35,058
And then you can essentially
work with adaptive solvers.

871
00:46:35,058 --> 00:46:37,761
Stiff solvers are more
for hard problems,

872
00:46:37,761 --> 00:46:39,863
like chaotic
systems, things that

873
00:46:39,863 --> 00:46:43,600
are oscillating, for example,
highly nonlinear systems,

874
00:46:43,600 --> 00:46:49,540
where you have to have different
strategies to solve differential

875
00:46:49,540 --> 00:46:50,274
equations.

876
00:46:53,010 --> 00:46:56,480
And you can actually read
this up on the benchmarks

877
00:46:56,480 --> 00:46:58,916
here, down here.

878
00:46:58,916 --> 00:47:01,685
The other thing that
we were very keen about

879
00:47:01,685 --> 00:47:04,488
is differentiability.

880
00:47:04,488 --> 00:47:10,060
Julia has a large, automated
differentiation ecosystem.

881
00:47:10,060 --> 00:47:11,595
And I don't know
how many people have

882
00:47:11,595 --> 00:47:15,799
heard of autodifferentiation.

883
00:47:15,799 --> 00:47:17,835
Some of you.

884
00:47:17,835 --> 00:47:21,872
So autodifferentiation, I
mean, you probably heard,

885
00:47:21,872 --> 00:47:25,008
clearly heard from machine
learning and neural networks

886
00:47:25,008 --> 00:47:27,177
that they call this
back propagation.

887
00:47:27,177 --> 00:47:31,982
Back propagation is essentially
reverse autodifferentiation.

888
00:47:31,982 --> 00:47:33,417
It's a different word.

889
00:47:33,417 --> 00:47:35,686
Different fields
use different words,

890
00:47:35,686 --> 00:47:39,456
but it's essentially
algorithmic, the same thing.

891
00:47:39,456 --> 00:47:42,392
The reason why I think
it's important to say

892
00:47:42,392 --> 00:47:44,528
automatic differentiation
is because there's also

893
00:47:44,528 --> 00:47:47,464
forward automatic
differentiation, which

894
00:47:47,464 --> 00:47:51,001
you use when you only
have about 100 parameters.

895
00:47:51,001 --> 00:47:52,636
Then actually, forward
differentiation

896
00:47:52,636 --> 00:47:56,273
is better than reverse
differentiation.

897
00:47:56,273 --> 00:47:59,977
Reverse autodifferentiation
is really effective

898
00:47:59,977 --> 00:48:01,545
when you have a
lot of parameters.

899
00:48:01,545 --> 00:48:03,013
So for example, in
neural networks,

900
00:48:03,013 --> 00:48:07,718
you have 10,000 or big models
have billions of parameters.

901
00:48:07,718 --> 00:48:11,288
Then back propagation is
really the method to do.

902
00:48:11,288 --> 00:48:14,725
And we have all these
libraries essentially

903
00:48:14,725 --> 00:48:18,595
in Julia, where you can
apply autodifferentiation

904
00:48:18,595 --> 00:48:21,231
to any function in Julia.

905
00:48:21,231 --> 00:48:28,538
I mean, maybe not any function,
but any function that is--

906
00:48:28,538 --> 00:48:31,208
it's like in math,
what is integrable

907
00:48:31,208 --> 00:48:34,678
and what is differentiable,
sort of like kinds of things.

908
00:48:34,678 --> 00:48:37,881
And the reason why we are so
excited about all the auto

909
00:48:37,881 --> 00:48:40,584
differentiable is
because then you

910
00:48:40,584 --> 00:48:43,687
can address problems that
you couldn't before address.

911
00:48:43,687 --> 00:48:47,357
One of the things is, what do
you need differentiability for?

912
00:48:47,357 --> 00:48:49,826
Optimization.

913
00:48:49,826 --> 00:48:53,697
So for example, if you
have a wonderful model

914
00:48:53,697 --> 00:48:57,167
that you just
discovered and vetted

915
00:48:57,167 --> 00:49:01,872
and it's the best model
that you can come up with,

916
00:49:01,872 --> 00:49:06,576
what you would like to do is to
then take data, patient data,

917
00:49:06,576 --> 00:49:11,548
and then fit parameters
to that particular model.

918
00:49:11,548 --> 00:49:13,817
And you can only do
this if you actually

919
00:49:13,817 --> 00:49:16,053
are good at differentiation.

920
00:49:16,053 --> 00:49:18,255
So optimization problem,
the inverse problem, that's

921
00:49:18,255 --> 00:49:20,057
the parameter fitting problem.

922
00:49:20,057 --> 00:49:23,360
And then there's
other applications

923
00:49:23,360 --> 00:49:26,697
that we're actually
pursuing in Neuroblox.

924
00:49:26,697 --> 00:49:29,733
And one is optimization
of the system itself.

925
00:49:29,733 --> 00:49:33,537
So let's say you have a
collection of neurons.

926
00:49:33,537 --> 00:49:36,740
You pick the neurons
because they are the ones

927
00:49:36,740 --> 00:49:37,908
that you want to use.

928
00:49:37,908 --> 00:49:40,677
But you need to optimize
them in terms of performance.

929
00:49:40,677 --> 00:49:42,045
You want the right firing rate.

930
00:49:42,045 --> 00:49:45,615
You want the right frequency
spectrum and stuff like this.

931
00:49:45,615 --> 00:49:49,486
And so what we can do is we
can essentially put constraints

932
00:49:49,486 --> 00:49:53,757
on the performance of
these collection of neurons

933
00:49:53,757 --> 00:49:57,728
and then do an optimization
process through optimization

934
00:49:57,728 --> 00:49:59,363
[INAUDIBLE].

935
00:49:59,363 --> 00:50:01,198
The other thing that
you can think of

936
00:50:01,198 --> 00:50:03,834
is then if you go
the Bayesian route,

937
00:50:03,834 --> 00:50:06,970
the Bayesian route would mean
that we don't necessarily

938
00:50:06,970 --> 00:50:09,373
do parameter fitting.

939
00:50:09,373 --> 00:50:11,208
We don't want to
fit best parameters.

940
00:50:11,208 --> 00:50:12,976
What we would like
to find the posterior

941
00:50:12,976 --> 00:50:16,480
probability of the
parameters given the data.

942
00:50:16,480 --> 00:50:19,016
Then you would go in
the Bayesian route.

943
00:50:19,016 --> 00:50:20,517
And when the Bayesian
route, there's

944
00:50:20,517 --> 00:50:26,957
a lot of packages that allow
you to do a Hamilton Monte Carlo

945
00:50:26,957 --> 00:50:29,826
on probability distributions.

946
00:50:29,826 --> 00:50:32,362
So that would give you the
full posterior probability

947
00:50:32,362 --> 00:50:33,530
of your data.

948
00:50:36,366 --> 00:50:38,935
So the other reason
why we picked Julia

949
00:50:38,935 --> 00:50:41,571
is because of composability.

950
00:50:41,571 --> 00:50:46,443
Julia has in part
of the suite of SciML

951
00:50:46,443 --> 00:50:48,678
tools, scientific
machine learning tools,

952
00:50:48,678 --> 00:50:54,418
has a package that's called
Modeling Toolkit, which

953
00:50:54,418 --> 00:50:59,389
uses symbolic equations to form
differential equation systems,

954
00:50:59,389 --> 00:51:00,490
ODE systems.

955
00:51:00,490 --> 00:51:03,427
And I just want to give
you a quick example.

956
00:51:03,427 --> 00:51:07,030
So these are actually
two blocks in Neuroblox.

957
00:51:07,030 --> 00:51:10,967
Each block is defined by
a differential equation.

958
00:51:10,967 --> 00:51:14,504
And you see there's a very
small junction in there,

959
00:51:14,504 --> 00:51:17,874
within the sigmoid function.

960
00:51:17,874 --> 00:51:20,277
There's a little
junction in here.

961
00:51:20,277 --> 00:51:22,412
And what you can do is
you can take, for example,

962
00:51:22,412 --> 00:51:24,581
the output of one
block and connect it

963
00:51:24,581 --> 00:51:26,383
to the junction
of the other one.

964
00:51:26,383 --> 00:51:29,453
And then you can connect
this x to the junction

965
00:51:29,453 --> 00:51:30,187
of the other one.

966
00:51:30,187 --> 00:51:32,122
And I indicated this
by these arrows.

967
00:51:32,122 --> 00:51:35,258
And that's exactly actually, how
you will see this in the GUI.

968
00:51:35,258 --> 00:51:39,262
Every arrow essentially means
that I connect an output of one

969
00:51:39,262 --> 00:51:43,400
of the ODE systems to an input
of the other ODE systems.

970
00:51:43,400 --> 00:51:45,602
And so what modeling
toolkit essentially does

971
00:51:45,602 --> 00:51:48,839
is it takes all these equations
and then cleans them up.

972
00:51:48,839 --> 00:51:51,408
It, for example,
removes the junctions.

973
00:51:51,408 --> 00:51:53,977
It eliminates the junctions
because the junction

974
00:51:53,977 --> 00:51:58,081
is a connection
and then turns that

975
00:51:58,081 --> 00:52:02,986
into a function that then can
be solved with an ODE solver.

976
00:52:02,986 --> 00:52:07,290
So this is full composability.

977
00:52:07,290 --> 00:52:12,496
And so the way we
constructed or designed

978
00:52:12,496 --> 00:52:15,732
Neuroblox is by using a
hierarchical and composable

979
00:52:15,732 --> 00:52:16,466
structure.

980
00:52:16,466 --> 00:52:20,203
So for example, you start
with a Hodgkin-Huxley neuron.

981
00:52:20,203 --> 00:52:23,807
You put five of those
or six of those neurons

982
00:52:23,807 --> 00:52:27,644
together into what we call
a winner takes all circuit.

983
00:52:27,644 --> 00:52:30,647
Actually, Lily was talking
about that exact circuit.

984
00:52:30,647 --> 00:52:33,617
And then you can take all
these winner takes all circuits

985
00:52:33,617 --> 00:52:36,987
and put them together and make,
for example, a cortical region

986
00:52:36,987 --> 00:52:38,388
and assemble them.

987
00:52:38,388 --> 00:52:40,891
Now, the nice thing about
this hierarchical structure

988
00:52:40,891 --> 00:52:43,493
is that you also have
hierarchical namespace.

989
00:52:43,493 --> 00:52:45,362
So if I have a
cortical region, I

990
00:52:45,362 --> 00:52:49,099
can ask, so give
me the first winner

991
00:52:49,099 --> 00:52:53,603
takes all circuit within
the cortical region

992
00:52:53,603 --> 00:52:57,240
and then give me the second
excitatory neuron in that.

993
00:52:57,240 --> 00:52:58,675
And that's essentially
a namespace

994
00:52:58,675 --> 00:52:59,843
that you can walk through.

995
00:52:59,843 --> 00:53:01,878
And I think in the
tutorials we will

996
00:53:01,878 --> 00:53:04,147
see exactly how to use this.

997
00:53:04,147 --> 00:53:06,016
We can also have
shared parameters.

998
00:53:06,016 --> 00:53:09,019
So you could, for example,
say we'll do a cortical region

999
00:53:09,019 --> 00:53:11,488
and all the parameters of
all the neurons are the same

1000
00:53:11,488 --> 00:53:13,256
and they are one number.

1001
00:53:13,256 --> 00:53:16,626
And then you can tune that
number rather than having

1002
00:53:16,626 --> 00:53:21,665
to tune 5,000
parameters at once.

1003
00:53:21,665 --> 00:53:23,867
We can also actually
define observables.

1004
00:53:23,867 --> 00:53:26,436
So we can put in
functions that then

1005
00:53:26,436 --> 00:53:28,371
can be later after
the simulation be

1006
00:53:28,371 --> 00:53:30,574
extracted from the system.

1007
00:53:30,574 --> 00:53:35,845
And actually, this is something
that Haris developed.

1008
00:53:35,845 --> 00:53:37,047
We have connection rules.

1009
00:53:37,047 --> 00:53:39,583
So if you have blocks and
you have other blocks,

1010
00:53:39,583 --> 00:53:41,785
then you can
essentially create rules

1011
00:53:41,785 --> 00:53:44,020
how to connect one
block to another block.

1012
00:53:44,020 --> 00:53:45,422
And it's very flexible.

1013
00:53:45,422 --> 00:53:47,624
And you will actually
learn during the tutorial,

1014
00:53:47,624 --> 00:53:52,295
I think, to make your own
blocks and your own connectors.

1015
00:53:52,295 --> 00:53:55,432
It's going to be
quite fun, actually.

1016
00:53:55,432 --> 00:53:58,802
I want to talk a little bit
about Neuroblox performance.

1017
00:53:58,802 --> 00:54:01,605
When we first
started this project,

1018
00:54:01,605 --> 00:54:05,408
we noticed that because we
start with symbolic equations,

1019
00:54:05,408 --> 00:54:07,711
it actually gets really slow.

1020
00:54:07,711 --> 00:54:10,213
So when you build
big systems, there's

1021
00:54:10,213 --> 00:54:12,148
a lot of simplifications
that has to be done.

1022
00:54:12,148 --> 00:54:14,050
And modeling
toolkit was actually

1023
00:54:14,050 --> 00:54:19,990
not up to the task to
give us fast compilation.

1024
00:54:19,990 --> 00:54:21,658
So what we noticed
actually that when

1025
00:54:21,658 --> 00:54:24,928
we have really large systems,
fully connected systems,

1026
00:54:24,928 --> 00:54:27,230
then the compilation
time is much larger

1027
00:54:27,230 --> 00:54:28,999
than the execution time.

1028
00:54:28,999 --> 00:54:30,066
And that's really bad.

1029
00:54:30,066 --> 00:54:34,004
So actually, Mason
was the driving force.

1030
00:54:34,004 --> 00:54:37,641
He developed a new way of
creating ODE systems, where

1031
00:54:37,641 --> 00:54:42,545
we take advantage of the fact
that we have the same thing

1032
00:54:42,545 --> 00:54:43,713
over and over.

1033
00:54:43,713 --> 00:54:45,482
So we have a
neuron, for example.

1034
00:54:45,482 --> 00:54:48,451
The neuron rules of connections
are always the same.

1035
00:54:48,451 --> 00:54:49,953
And then you can
essentially take

1036
00:54:49,953 --> 00:54:52,722
advantage of that structure.

1037
00:54:52,722 --> 00:54:55,058
Actually, what
really would we take

1038
00:54:55,058 --> 00:54:57,594
advantage of is that every
system that we create

1039
00:54:57,594 --> 00:55:00,930
is actually a graph,
because you have nodes.

1040
00:55:00,930 --> 00:55:04,401
You have edges and the edges
define the connections.

1041
00:55:04,401 --> 00:55:08,705
And creating equations out of
graphs is much more effective.

1042
00:55:08,705 --> 00:55:12,008
And so we created actually
a library: Graph Dynamics,

1043
00:55:12,008 --> 00:55:15,211
that compiles graph systems
much more efficiently.

1044
00:55:15,211 --> 00:55:19,149
And you can see Modeling
Toolkit is on the-- this

1045
00:55:19,149 --> 00:55:21,818
is the blue graph, blue data.

1046
00:55:21,818 --> 00:55:25,088
And Graph Dynamics is red.

1047
00:55:25,088 --> 00:55:27,123
And so we are, again,
orders of magnitude

1048
00:55:27,123 --> 00:55:31,094
faster than that
modeling toolkit.

1049
00:55:31,094 --> 00:55:35,932
And I conclude by saying
that we essentially

1050
00:55:35,932 --> 00:55:37,367
have the following features.

1051
00:55:37,367 --> 00:55:40,136
We are composable, hierarchical.

1052
00:55:40,136 --> 00:55:44,941
We can build neuroscience models
from computational primitives.

1053
00:55:44,941 --> 00:55:47,310
We have a native high
performance computing language.

1054
00:55:47,310 --> 00:55:49,346
I mean, Julia-- by the
way, I didn't say this.

1055
00:55:49,346 --> 00:55:51,848
Julia is about 10 years old.

1056
00:55:51,848 --> 00:55:54,951
It was developed here at MIT in
the math and computer science

1057
00:55:54,951 --> 00:55:55,952
department.

1058
00:55:55,952 --> 00:56:01,124
And it's actually a
language from mathematicians

1059
00:56:01,124 --> 00:56:03,326
for mathematicians.

1060
00:56:03,326 --> 00:56:05,028
But engineers can also use it.

1061
00:56:05,028 --> 00:56:08,431
It's, I think, fine.

1062
00:56:08,431 --> 00:56:10,533
But it's designed for high
performance computing,

1063
00:56:10,533 --> 00:56:16,439
so interface with GPUs
and multi-core CPUs.

1064
00:56:16,439 --> 00:56:22,212
By the way, JuliaHub is a way
of running Julia in the cloud.

1065
00:56:22,212 --> 00:56:24,280
And you can throw essentially
any computing power

1066
00:56:24,280 --> 00:56:26,850
on your problem.

1067
00:56:26,850 --> 00:56:30,487
So we have differential models
for optimization parameter

1068
00:56:30,487 --> 00:56:31,821
fitting.

1069
00:56:31,821 --> 00:56:36,092
That also then allows us to do
computational model comparisons,

1070
00:56:36,092 --> 00:56:39,963
because if you have two models,
they're all differentiable

1071
00:56:39,963 --> 00:56:42,365
and we can do parameter fitting,
then you can essentially

1072
00:56:42,365 --> 00:56:45,902
do Bayesian posterior ratios.

1073
00:56:45,902 --> 00:56:48,705
And then you can essentially
tell which model is better.

1074
00:56:48,705 --> 00:56:51,408
And that's actually one
of our plans in Neuroblox

1075
00:56:51,408 --> 00:56:57,280
that we could essentially have
people submit different models

1076
00:56:57,280 --> 00:56:58,648
and then we can rank them.

1077
00:56:58,648 --> 00:57:01,217
We can say, OK, so how well
do they do with respect

1078
00:57:01,217 --> 00:57:04,387
to the data that are available?

1079
00:57:04,387 --> 00:57:08,458
And so we also will have a
graphic user interface, which I

1080
00:57:08,458 --> 00:57:12,295
will present right after this.

1081
00:57:12,295 --> 00:57:13,997
Thank you so much.

